  "transcript": "# Former His Majesty’s Chief Inspector of Constabulary and Fire and Rescue Services\n\n## Role and achievements\n> Sir Andy explains his priorities as chief inspector: independence, an outcome focus, and pressing for better neighbourhood policing.\n\nChair: Sir Andy, thank you for coming. Could you briefly say who you are and what you consider your main achievements as chief inspector?\nSir Andy Cooke: I was HM Chief Inspector of Constabulary and Fire and Rescue Services until the end of March 2026. Internally I’m proud of building a cohesive inspectorate with high staff morale. Externally I kept the role sensibly independent, pushed hard for neighbourhood policing and held forces to account — placing more forces into special measures than before where standards were low.\nChair: You moved the inspectorate away from tick-box checks. What did you focus on instead?\nSir Andy Cooke: I shifted to an outcome-based approach: inspections assess whether victims are helped, crimes properly investigated, and problems solved — not whether there’s a glossy plan on a shelf. That drove a rise in some performance areas.\n\n\n## Getting the basics right\n> The public’s confidence depends on routine policing: answering calls, quick attendance, proper investigation and keeping victims informed.\n\nChair: You’ve emphasised the ‘basics’. What are they, and how well are forces doing them?\nSir Andy Cooke: By basics I mean answering the phone quickly, giving correct advice, attending swiftly, preserving the ‘golden hour’ for evidence, investigating properly and keeping victims informed. Some forces do these well, others badly — it’s a postcode lottery.\nChair: Has the focus on neighbourhood policing reduced investigative capacity?\nSir Andy Cooke: It shouldn’t. A neighbourhood officer should have an investigative mindset. Neighbourhood policing is the bedrock: visible, known officers who act to prevent and solve local problems. Too often we’ve seen inexperience, weak supervision and leadership gaps causing failures on the basics.\nChair: Is underperformance mainly about money or about capability?\nSir Andy Cooke: Both. You get the police you pay for, but just adding money won’t fix everything. We need structural change, better use of technology and stronger leadership as well as resources.\n\n\n## Recruitment, retention and leadership\n> Rapid recruitment produced numbers but not always the right experience; retention is weak and senior appointment processes need reform.\n\nChair: You mentioned recruitment and retention. What went wrong with the uplift and why are people leaving?\nSir Andy Cooke: The uplift returned officers lost after austerity and was necessary, but it was rushed. Some recruits weren’t the right fit and many younger officers are leaving within five years. Workload and stress are big factors; inexperienced supervisors compound problems.\nChair: What about leadership at the top — why are so many chiefs suspended or failing?\nSir Andy Cooke: Being a chief is demanding but not impossible. The problem is how chiefs are appointed: one person (a PCC) often selects them, producing small candidate fields and too many home-grown appointments. The White Paper proposes reintroducing a senior appointments panel to broaden and scrutinise choices — I support that.\nChair: Should there be minimum frontline experience for those promoted into senior roles?\nSir Andy Cooke: Yes. Frontline experience matters. Direct entry to senior ranks has produced a few successes but many failures. Leaders should have real operational credibility and more structured leadership training throughout their careers.\n\n\n## Funding and the White Paper reforms\n> Sir Andy backs major White Paper changes — a National Police Service, fewer forces and a fairer funding formula — but stresses local policing must be protected.\n\nChair: Tell us your view on the White Paper and the National Police Service proposal.\nSir Andy Cooke: I support most of it. England and Wales have 43 forces; some are too small for modern threats. A National Police Service can coordinate major functions — procurement, IT, counter-terror and nationally important responses — and should reduce waste and raise standards.\nChair: I represent a border constituency and worry about losing local control. How do you protect local policing?\nSir Andy Cooke: You must set up strong local policing areas led by local chief superintendents with clear governance — mayors or local councils involved — so day-to-day policing stays local while national functions are centralised.\nChair: Is the funding model fair now?\nSir Andy Cooke: No. The current formula relies too much on local council tax and produces big inequities — wealthy council-tax areas get more. The White Paper proposes reviewing the funding formula and that needs to happen.\n\n\n## Technology, data and AI\n> Policing holds lots of data but needs modern, unified systems and cautious, human-supervised use of AI; legacy IT is a major drain.\n\nChair: You’ve said policing is 'barely in the foothills' of data insight. What stops them using data better?\nSir Andy Cooke: Policing has fragmented legacy systems bought by 43 forces with different needs; data doesn’t talk across them. Nearly all IT spend goes on running old systems, leaving little to innovate. A national purchaser would give better value and consistency.\nChair: What about AI and facial recognition — are there legal and ethical safeguards?\nSir Andy Cooke: There must be statutory clarity and a human in the loop. Don’t arrest someone solely on an algorithm. For facial recognition, the public worry about stores of faces — in practice a match should prompt further enquiries, not automatic action.\nChair: Can AI help reduce officer workload?\nSir Andy Cooke: Yes, if used safely and sovereignly. The goal is to free officers from data entry so they spend more time on patrol. But you need proper investment, built-for-policing systems and strong governance to stop misuse.\n\n\n## Complaints, misconduct and final message\n> Vetting, timelier misconduct investigations and national learning are essential; Sir Andy asks for political and public support for officers.\n\nChair: On misconduct and complaints: is the system working and is the IOPC effective?\nSir Andy Cooke: The IOPC has improved recently and its director general is doing good work. Professional standards departments can be slow and sometimes give the impression of bias. Using other forces to investigate serious complaints can help, and ongoing vetting must be stronger.\nChair: How do we make sure lessons from scandals are learned nationally?\nSir Andy Cooke: The inspectorate must follow up lessons, and forces must stop being insular. When a problem is found in one place, others must adopt fixes rather than saying, 'That’s how we do it here.' A National Police Service can help spread learning.\nChair: Any final point you want the committee to take away?\nSir Andy Cooke: Support the police — they get things right 99% of the time in difficult conditions. Fix the basics, reform appointments and funding, invest in modern IT with guarded use of AI, and protect frontline welfare so officers can do the job communities need.\n",
